Z||000000||BAM-BAM||05-31-2000||09:08 PM||||  I know I have seen other post's on this topic but never relly paid attention... because it wasn't my problem; now it is my problem. I was driving around town today, Las Vegas, sunny and about 108. The temp guage never went above 200. When I stopped and turned it off it wouldn't restart. It wouldn't even turn the starter; good thing I was near my wifes work, I just used her Grand Prix. Now, she gets off of work and calls to tell me, it started. What could this be? What part of the ignition system is heat sensitive? I was thinking maybe  the ignition module? The plugs, cap, rotor, IAC, TPS, O2, EGR tube, EGR reg, are all new. Never had a hint of a problem before. TIA for and advise/help.<P> Thanx, BAM-BAM  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||5
Z||000001||Ozzy||06-01-2000||01:35 AM||||I wouldn't think it would be anything related to sensors or ign.  module if the starter would not turn. I would start looking for poor quality battery cable connections. Poor current to starter solenoid?? Poor battery ground exacerbated by excess heat? Was the A/C turned on while trying to re-start? <P>Ozzy<p>[This message has been edited by Ozzy (edited 06-01-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Oreif||06-01-2000||02:25 AM||||It is commonly the starter. The starter solenoid quits working when it gets hot. Wait 20 - 30 minutes it cools down and car starts fine. Easy way to check is get some "freeze spray" from a electronics store. Drive car around for a while then stop car and see if it will restart. If it doesn't spray the solenoid on top of the starter a few moments and see if it starts. It will take some time to cool it down even with the freeze spray but you'll know for sure if it's the starter.||||reg||1
